{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And you bring near to yourself Aaron your brother from the midst of the children of Israel.</b> Here the expression ״וְאַתָּה״ is added, to tell you that on account of the deed of the calf Aaron was distanced, just as the firstborn of Israel were disqualified, and Moses brought him near through his prayer, as it is written (Deuteronomy 9:20) ״וּבְאַהֲרֹן הִתְאַנַּף ה׳ מְאֹד לְהַשְׁמִידוֹ וָאֶתְפַּלֵּל גַּם בְּעַד אַהֲרֹן בָּעֵת הַהִיא״. And the nature of this prayer was that Moses bound it to himself, saying: is he not my brother and my flesh? and his destruction is as though half my flesh were consumed — in the manner that he prayed for Miriam. It follows that because Moses brought him near to himself, therefore he was chosen by the merit of Moses, even though he too was among the children of Israel who worshipped the calf; nevertheless He brought him near from among the children of Israel who worshipped — and all is for your sake. Therefore it says ״וְאַתָּה הַקְרֵב אֵלֶיךָ אֶת אַהֲרֹן מִתּוֹךְ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״." "Another matter: therefore it says ״מִתּוֹךְ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, because prophecy does not rest upon the prophets of Israel except by the merit of Israel, as Rashi explained in the portion of Deuteronomy on the verse ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֵלַי״ (Deuteronomy 2:17). And this comes to say that even though Aaron was worthy of being distanced, nevertheless HaShem brought him near on account of two things: the one, for the sake of Moses, for he is his brother and the honor of Aaron is the honor of Moses; and the second, by the merit of the children of Israel, who needed such a priest — a pursuer of peace. Therefore both of them are stated: ״וְאַתָּה הַקְרֵב אֵלֶיךָ״, ״וּמִתּוֹךְ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:44.233164407Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}